<article class="env-example" id="0EL3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EL3">Example <span data-tag="0EL3">52.28.3</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field and let $X$ be a proper variety over $k$. Let $Y \subset X$ be an effective Cartier divisor such that $\mathcal{O}_ X(Y)$ is ample and denote $Y_ n$ its $n$th infinitesimal neighbourhood. Let $\mathcal{E}$ be a finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. Here are some special cases of Proposition <a href="/tag/0EL1" data-tag="0EL1">52.28.1</a>. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $X$ is a curve, we don't learn anything. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $X$ is a Cohen-Macaulay (for example normal) surface, then 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  H^0(X, \mathcal{E}) \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^0(Y_ n, \mathcal{E}|_{Y_ n})  \]
</div>
<p>
 is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $X$ is a Cohen-Macaulay threefold, then 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  H^0(X, \mathcal{E}) \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^0(Y_ n, \mathcal{E}|_{Y_ n}) \quad \text{and}\quad H^1(X, \mathcal{E}) \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^1(Y_ n, \mathcal{E}|_{Y_ n})  \]
</div>
<p>
 are isomorphisms. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Presumably the pattern is clear. If $X$ is a normal threefold, then we can conclude the result for $H^0$ but not for $H^1$. </p>
</article>